{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1795543748,"nonce":"EUFLKnGhrO2Etktq6R7i9/4LtXY=","target":"859bab200b5713471d66a34f8060ebf2843a25026779d04d66b7dc412590a469","message":"`jno.solve.imex` has no test on a detonation, so the combination of shocks, stiff chemistry and IMEX time stepping is untested. The reference is the Chapman-Jouguet speed of a 1-D reacting-Euler detonation.\n\n**Covered today.** `tests/test_solve_imex.py` checks a 0-D ignition against scipy `Radau` (`test_imex_ignites_when_the_stiff_ode_does`), an algebraic temperature solved with the local terms (`test_an_algebraic_temperature_is_solved_with_the_local_terms`), and a premixed flame speed against the phase-plane eigenvalue (`test_a_premixed_flame_runs_at_the_travelling_wave_speed`). These have no shock.\n\n**Expected.** A steadily travelling detonation in a 1-D reacting-Euler `jno.fvm` problem propagates at the Chapman-Jouguet speed, which follows from the Rankine-Hugoniot relations and the sonic condition at the end of reaction. The measured front speed should match it to within the resolution error, and a ZND profile gives the structure behind the front.\n\n**Why deferred.** The test needs a resolved ZND zone, so a long, fine march. That is too expensive for the regular test run in #345.\n\n**Direction.** Add a slow-marked test (or a documented benchmark) with a one-step irreversible reaction, a fine grid in the reaction zone, and a front-speed measurement against the closed-form CJ speed. Open questions: the grid and run length that resolve the zone at an acceptable cost, and whether the test belongs in the regular suite or among the benchmarks.","files":null}]}